---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const repo = "https://github.com/zalify/offdesk";
---

<Site
  title="Offdesk behind a Cloudflare Tunnel"
  description="Reach the hub at home from anywhere, with no port opened: the full Cloudflare Tunnel setup for Offdesk."
  path="/tunnel"
>
  <article class="doc mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pt-12 pb-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">Away from home</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-3 text-4xl md:text-5xl">A Cloudflare Tunnel</h1>
    <p class="prose-lead mt-6">
      For a hub on a NAS or a desktop at home. <code>cloudflared</code> runs
      next to the hub and dials <em>out</em> to Cloudflare, which then serves
      your hostname over HTTPS. No port forwarding, no dynamic DNS, no
      certificate to renew, and it works behind CGNAT where forwarding a port
      is not even possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      For ordinary browser connections, Cloudflare terminates HTTPS and can
      read terminal traffic. Encrypted App pairing adds encryption between the
      App and Hub, so the tunnel carries encrypted terminal content. Follow the
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/encrypted-connections.md`}>encrypted connection guide</a>
      for that mode. You can also use a
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/setup-public.md#tailscale`}>tailnet</a>
      instead: same binaries, nobody in the middle.
    </p>
    <p>You need a domain on a Cloudflare account. The free plan is enough.</p>

    <h2>1. Create the tunnel</h2>
    <p>
      Cloudflare Zero Trust dashboard → Networks → Tunnels → Create a tunnel →
      <strong>Cloudflared</strong>. Name it, then copy the token it shows you —
      the long string after <code>--token</code> in the install command. That
      token is a credential: anyone holding it can serve traffic as your tunnel.
    </p>
    <p>Add a public hostname to the tunnel:</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Subdomain / domain</td><td><code>Offdesk</code> / <code>example.com</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Service type</td><td><code>HTTP</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>URL</td><td><code>server:4317</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>server</code> is the hub's service name in
      <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, which is how <code>cloudflared</code>
      reaches it over the compose network. The hop from Cloudflare to your
      machine is inside the tunnel; the hop from <code>cloudflared</code> to
      the hub never leaves the Docker network, so plain HTTP there is fine.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Hub</h2>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/zalify/offdesk && cd offdesk</code></pre>
    <p><code>.env</code> next to <code>docker-compose.yml</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>JWT_SECRET=&lt;output of: openssl rand -hex 32&gt;
OFFDESK_BASE_URL=https://offdesk.example.com
TUNNEL_TOKEN=&lt;the token from step 1&gt;
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=</code></pre>
    <pre><code>chmod 600 .env</code></pre>
    <p>
      A hub on the internet signs in through GitHub or Google. Register the
      OAuth callback URLs exactly as in
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/setup-public.md#2-oauth-callback-urls`}>the VPS guide</a>
      — the hub builds them from <code>OFFDESK_BASE_URL</code>, and nothing
      about a tunnel changes that. Then:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose --profile tunnel up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p>
      The <code>tunnel</code> profile is what starts <code>cloudflared</code>;
      without it you get the hub alone, still bound to
      <code>127.0.0.1:4317</code>. Check both are up:
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose --profile tunnel ps
curl -sf -o /dev/null -w "%&#123;http_code&#125;\n" https://offdesk.example.com/</code></pre>

    <h2>3. Machines</h2>
    <p>
      Open the hub in a browser and choose <strong>Add host</strong>: it shows
      one line with a registration token filled in. On the machine you want to
      reach:
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://offdesk.dev/install | sh -s -- --hub-url wss://offdesk.example.com/ws/machine --token &lt;token&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      A machine on the same LAN as the hub still goes out to Cloudflare and
      back. If that bothers you, register it with the hub's LAN address instead
      — the machine agent only needs to reach the hub, and it does not care
      that your phone reaches the same hub by a different name.
    </p>

    <h2>Notes</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign-in is open to anyone who finds the URL</strong>, same as
        the VPS path: any GitHub or Google account can create an account on your
        hub. There is no allowlist. Cloudflare Access can put a second login in
        front of the whole hostname, but everything that is not a browser —
        <code>offdesk-node</code>, the CLI, the Android app — then needs an
        Access service token of its own, and the machine agent has nowhere to
        put one. Either scope the Access policy to the browser paths, or leave
        it off and rely on the hub's own sign-in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Long-lived WebSockets are fine.</strong> Machine agents ping
        every 30 seconds and browsers ping at the application layer, so idle
        timeouts along the path do not silently drop terminals.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Do not use a quick tunnel</strong> (<code>cloudflared tunnel --url</code>,
        the <code>*.trycloudflare.com</code> hostnames) for anything but a
        demo. The hostname changes every restart, which invalidates your OAuth
        callback URLs, every registered machine, and any phone that has the
        old one saved.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-10">
      The other two routes — a VPS behind Caddy, and Tailscale — with the
      trade-offs between all three:
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/setup-public.md`}>docs/setup-public.md</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Site>
